        <TR>
          <TD vAlign=3Dtop height=3D125>
            <P class=3Dheadline>Planning a BBQ</P>
            <TABLE cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"90%" =
border=3D0>
              <TBODY>
              <TR>
                <TD vAlign=3Dtop>
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Outdoor Barbecuing for =
Groups</P>Outdoor=20
                  cooking or barbecuing to feed small and large groups =
is one of=20
                  the most popular activities for 4-H and civic fund =
raisers,=20
                  and for supporting educational meetings, church =
functions and=20
                  field days. The sociability of the meal and the ease =
of=20
                  service count almost as much as how the food tastes =
and=20
                  provide excellent public relations for your =
organization. Care=20
                  must be taken to ensure that quality food in the right =

                  quantity is prepared and that proper precautions are =
taken to=20
                  prevent food poisoning. Following a few simple steps =
will=20
                  ensure the meal is appealing, is served on time, is =
hot (or=20
                  cold), is available in sufficient quantity and is =
delicious.=20
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Planning the Menu</P>The =
successful=20
                  management of an outdoor meal brings satisfaction to =
everyone=20
                  involved and reflects the thoughtful preparation and =
planning=20
                  of your organization. Effective planning means =
dividing the=20
                  work and assigning the responsibilities for getting =
the job=20
                  done. A general planning committee with smaller work=20
                  committees for food preparation, site and facilities, =
cleanup=20
                  and publicity best accomplishes this. The size of =
these=20
                  committees varies with the type of meal and the number =
of=20
                  people served. Planning is the key to a smoothly =
functioning=20
                  activity. The major items, such as menu, price, site =
and type=20
                  of service, should be planned well in advance. =
Complete=20
                  records should be kept for financial purposes and so =
that the=20
                  event can be evaluated with suggestions recorded for =
future=20
                  reference.=20
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Worksheets</P>Develop summary =
worksheets=20
                  to ensure that all needs are met. Prepare a market =
order from=20
                  the menu, showing the serving size, number of portions =
and=20
                  total amount to be prepared. This will prevent =
shortages and=20
                  surpluses. Prepare a market order for all materials, =
paper=20
                  goods, pit requirements and cleanup materials. Make a =
list of=20
                  equipment needed to prepare, cook and serve each item. =
Assign=20
                  specific duties and responsibilities to workers or =
volunteers=20
                  so that all jobs are completed.=20
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Menu Selection</P>There are =
several key=20
                  considerations in selecting a menu for an outdoor =
event. Plan=20
                  a complete menu to be sure the meal is nutritionally =
balanced.=20
                  Keep the menu simple. Simple menus are most =
effectively and=20
                  efficiently served to larger groups. Serving sizes =
should be=20
                  slightly larger to satisfy the heartier outdoor =
appetite. Cost=20
                  is always a consideration but should not jeopardize =
the=20
                  success of the event. Carefully calculate the meal =
cost and=20
                  consider low cost items such as corn on the cob, grits =
or=20
                  rice, biscuits or corn bread, and fresh fruits and =
vegetables=20
                  in season. Include variety in texture, color, flavor =
and=20
                  temperature in the menu. Bakery items work well as =
desserts=20
                  for large groups because they can be prepared ahead of =
time=20
                  and are easily served. However, for smaller groups, =
fruits in=20
                  season, ice cream or a combination of the two add =
flair and=20
                  should be considered. Congealed or gelatin desserts do =
not=20
                  work well. Be sure to account for the differences in =
age, sex=20
                  and occupation of the group. Serve hearty foods to =
people who=20
                  do heavy physical work and consider lighter or fancier =
food=20
                  for women's groups. When planning the menu, consider =
the time=20
                  of day the meal will be served, the time required for =
service,=20
                  the time required for food preparation and meetings =
that=20
                  follow the meal and that require your involvement. =
Don't be=20
                  afraid to add a little flair to the menu as long as =
you use=20
                  standard, pre-tested recipes and consider the skill =
required=20
                  to prepare the dish.=20
                  <P>Food safety is important in planning the menu.=20
                  Refrigeration and cooking facilities available at the =
chosen=20
                  location may dictate the use of non-perishable items =
such as=20
                  potato chips or limit the number of refrigerated items =

                  served.</P>
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Labor Requirement</P>The amount =
of time=20
                  required for each task associated with an outdoor meal =
is=20
                  often under-estimated. Assign individuals specific =
duties for=20
                  the following tasks:=20
                  <UL>
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Assembling the cooking =
equipment=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Obtaining the food and =
serving=20
                    materials=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Preparing the barbecue pit=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Preparing the food=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Six high output burners =
behind=20
                    eight-foot smoker=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Serving the food=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Cleaning up and disposing of =
waste=20
                    </LI></UL>Task assignments should be made even for =
small=20
                  groups to ensure that the work is accomplished.=20
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Site Selection</P>The type of =
menu to be=20
                  served and the type of service (picnic, cafeteria, =
take out or=20
                  banquet style) influence the facility you choose. =
Large=20
                  outdoor pit barbecues do not require extensive =
seating, but=20
                  well organized serving facilities must be planned. =
When=20
                  meetings are combined with or follow the meal, seating =
must be=20
                  provided. In case of inclement weather, alternative =
facilities=20
                  large enough to accommodate the group should be =
planned or=20
                  alternative dates chosen. Overflow crowds can be a =
problem if=20
                  plans do not include additional facilities. Advance=20
                  registration or ticket sales can assist in planning =
the site=20
                  and the amount of food to prepare. Past records can =
provide an=20
                  estimate of the number to be served if advance tickets =
cannot=20
                  be sold.=20
                  <P>Locate the pit or grill in a well-ventilated area =
where the=20
                  fumes and smoke will not accumulate in the building or =

                  facility to be used for seating. Be sure the site is=20
                  accessible and has adequate parking and restroom =
facilities.=20
                  All publicity should contain directions to the =
function. Plan=20
                  access to the facility far enough in advance for setup =
and=20
                  cooking to be accomplished before the meal is =
scheduled to be=20
                  served.</P>
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Food Purchasing</P>Before =
purchasing food=20
                  items, prepare a market order with the estimated =
portion size,=20
                  number to be served and total amount to purchase. =
Follow=20
                  standardized. Tested recipes. Small quantity recipes =
are often=20
                  not accurate when used in multiple quantities. Table 2 =
and=20
                  Table 3 will assist you in determining the correct =
amount of=20
                  food to be purchased for the number to be served. Be =
sure to=20
                  have enough food, but avoid excesses, which increase =
the total=20
                  cost. Control serving size using scoops, spoons or =
ladles to=20
                  ensure you serve the expected number of people. If=20
                  self-service is used, plan for larger portions.=20
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Meat Selection and =
Cookery</P>Barbecuing=20
                  and charcoal grilling are dry heat methods of cooking. =
Select=20
                  tender cuts of meat such as the rib, loin, sirloin or =
large=20
                  roast from the rump and round or leg cuts (see Table =
1). The=20
                  age of the animal has the greatest effect on =
tenderness, so=20
                  select lamb or kid goat. The USDA grade can be used as =
a=20
                  purchasing guide when selecting beef or lamb. Most =
swine is=20
                  slaughtered for pork at less than six months of age, =
so=20
                  toughness due to age is not a problem. The size of the =
cut=20
                  affects the rate of cooking and the ultimate =
tenderness and=20
                  juiciness of the meat. Select steaks and chops one to =
1-1/2=20
                  inches thick and cook them slowly over a medium fire =
for best=20
                  results. It is better to cook one thick steak and =
carve it=20
                  into portions than to cook several thin steaks. Larger =
roasts=20
                  make excellent barbecue meats because of the smaller =
surface=20
                  area in relation to the weight of the cut. This allows =
a=20
                  slowly cooked piece of meat to tenderize in its own =
juices=20
                  during the cooking process. Cuts should be uniform in =
size and=20
                  shape so that cooking times will be approximately the =
same.=20
                  Roast thickness should not exceed five inches. Less =
tender=20
                  cuts such as pork spare ribs and beef ribs can be =
successfully=20
                  grilled by first steaming or parboiling until the =
connective=20
                  tissue in the meat is broken down and then grilling =
the meat=20
                  to achieve browning and a char broiled flavor. Less =
tender=20
                  steaks can be tenderized prior to grilling. To =
tenderize the=20
                  meat, use a commercial meat tenderizer or marinate the =
meat=20
                  using a tested marinade recipe. Always marinate meats =
in the=20
                  refrigerator. The fat and marbling in meat is =
important for=20
                  barbecuing. The fat protects the meat from dehydration =
during=20
                  cooking, increases the rate of cooking and contributes =
to the=20
                  juiciness and flavor of the final product. Trim the =
outside=20
                  fat, but leave a uniform cover over the surface of the =
meat=20
                  during cooking. Some fat will render out during =
cooking and=20
                  leave a tender, juicy piece of meat when done. The =
outside fat=20
                  can be removed before serving or removed on the plate. =
The=20
                  outside fat should be scored on steaks and chops to =
prevent=20
                  the meat from curling on the grill.=20
                  <P>Meat flavor comes not only from the type of fat =
(beef,=20
                  lamb, pork) but also from the browning reaction =
achieved=20
                  during cooking. The unique barbecued flavor results =
from the=20
                  browning of the fat and protein on the surface of the =
product.=20
                  Smoke flavor is absorbed into the meat along with any =
spices,=20
                  seasoning and flavoring that are added before, during =
or after=20
                  barbecuing.</P>
                  <P>Frozen roasts and steaks can be used for barbecuing =
or=20
                  charcoal broiling; however, expect a dryer finished =
product=20
                  due to the increased drip loss that occurs during the =
cooking=20
                  of frozen meat. For best results, thaw roasts and =
steaks=20
                  before cooking. Always thaw meat under refrigeration, =
never at=20
                  room temperature. Microwave thawing is the easiest, =
safest=20
                  method available. Hamburgers can be cooked from the =
frozen=20
                  state to reduce the amount of bacteria growth that may =
occur=20
                  during thawing. To reduce juice loss during cooking, =
turn=20
                  steaks and chops with tongs rather than using a fork =
and never=20
                  press hamburger patties on the grill while cooking. =
Basting=20
                  meat cuts during cooking greatly reduces the amount of =
surface=20
                  moisture lost and allows you to add a flavoring spice =
to the=20
                  meat. Use salt sparingly during cooking, because salt =
draws=20
                  the moisture out of the meat. Basting also improves =
the color=20
                  and yield of the barbecued cuts.</P>
                  <P>The only sure way to determine when meat is done is =
to use=20
                  a meat thermometer. To check the temperature, insert =
the tip=20
                  of the thermometer into the center of the thickest =
part of the=20
                  cut without touching a fat pocket or bone. The end =
point=20
                  internal temperature for the degrees of doneness for =
meat=20
                  is:</P>
                  <P></P>
                  <DL>
                    <DD>145 degrees F rare=20
                    <DD>160 degrees F medium=20
                    <DD>170 degrees F well </DD></DL>
                  <P>Whole poultry should be cooked to an internal =
temperature=20
                  of 180 degrees F. The temperature should be checked in =
the=20
                  thickest part of the thigh. For poultry parts, the =
thickest=20
                  part of the meat should reach 180 degrees F for dark =
meat and=20
                  170 degrees F for white meat. Best results are =
achieved when=20
                  whole pork primal cuts (hams, shoulders, butts) are =
cooked to=20
                  an internal temperature of 165 degrees F. Cured pork =
chops or=20
                  ham steaks need to cook to only 145 degrees F because =
they=20
                  have been cooked during processing.<BR>When cooking =
whole=20
                  hogs, pigs or pork sides, it is recommended to leave =
the skin=20
                  and fat on during barbecuing. This reduces shrink and=20
                  increases heat penetration during cooking. The fat and =
skin=20
                  can easily be removed during preparation, and the =
skins can be=20
                  popped in hot (400 degrees F) fat and served as a side =

                  dish.</P>
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Purchasing Dry Goods and Paper=20
                  Materials</P>Dry goods and paper materials are often =
sold in=20
                  quantities of 50 and 100 and can be stored for future =
use.=20
                  These items can be purchased well in advance, freeing =
time=20
                  closer to the event for meal preparation. Be sure to =
store all=20
                  dry materials in a cool dry area free of rats, mice =
and=20
                  roaches. Large plastic bags can protect these =
materials from=20
                  becoming soiled. Be sure to use the worksheet to list =
all of=20
                  the items needed, the quantity and cost. This provides =
a=20
                  checklist to ensure all necessary items are on hand =
and aids=20
                  in planning future events. Store all unused materials =
and=20
                  inventory them for future reference.=20
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Setting a Price for the =
Meal</P>Unless a=20
                  meal has a sponsor, you should set the price high =
enough to=20
                  cover all costs and allow for a profit if profit is an =

                  objective. Here are some guidelines for estimating =
costs:=20
                  <UL>
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Use the worksheet to price =
all food=20
                    items, including donated items, and total their =
cost.=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Add the cost of labor, if =
any, to the=20
                    food cost and include paper goods and miscellaneous =
costs=20
                    such as those for decorating, non-paying guest =
meals,=20
                    laundry or rental costs and janitor services. This =
is the=20
                    estimated total meal cost.=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Divide the estimated total =
meal cost=20
                    by the estimated number of people to be served =
(including=20
                    volunteer workers). This will give you the =
approximate meal=20
                    cost per person. </LI></UL>When the meal is served =
at cost,=20
                  charge only enough to ensure that you do not lose =
money. Allow=20
                  a margin of safety to cover changes in the number of =
people=20
                  served, and then multiply the estimated cost by 125 =
percent.=20
                  For example, if the estimated meal cost is $2.00, =
charge $2.50=20
                  per person. When the meal is served to earn money, a =
fair=20
                  profit is calculated by using the cost as 50 percent =
of the=20
                  meal price. Estimate all costs (not including labor) =
and=20
                  divide by 0.50 (percentage meal cost) to determine the =
selling=20
                  price. Round to the nearest $0.25. <BR>
                  <DIV style=3D"MARGIN-LEFT: 2em">Example: Estimated =
food cost=20
                  $206.50<BR>Decoration and paper goods =
18.50<BR>Facility rental=20
                  25.00<BR>Total estimated cost =3D $250.00<BR>Estimated =
number of=20
                  people 100<BR>Estimated cost per person=20
                  $250/100=3D$2.50<BR>Selling price =
$2.50/0.5=3D$5.00<BR></DIV>
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Food Preparation Equipment and=20
                  Containers</P>A carefully prepared worksheet listing =
the=20
                  equipment required to prepare and cook each menu item =
will=20
                  ensure that sufficient equipment, containers and =
cooking=20
                  utensils are available. The grill or pit area needed =
should be=20
                  calculated with consideration given to the time =
required for=20
                  cooking and for serving the food hot. If the meal is =
to be=20
                  served over a three-hour period, smaller items such as =
chicken=20
                  halves can be prepared on the same grill at a later =
time.=20
                  However, larger meat cuts require longer cooking times =
and=20
                  generally will be cooked all together. Allow grill =
space to=20
                  cook four steaks per square foot, or 2-1/2 chicken =
halves per=20
                  square foot.<BR>Include foils zip lock bags or plastic =

                  containers with lids on the worksheet, to provide for =
handling=20
                  and storing leftovers. Often, a successful barbecue =
will=20
                  generate sales for the leftover meat or hash you have =
on hand.=20
                  Be sure you have made your meal cost calculations =
beforehand=20
                  so you can properly price the product. Roaster ovens =
are=20
                  excellent for cooking baked beans, hash or other foods =
that=20
                  are served hot. Fish fryers can be used for preparing =
grits,=20
                  rice, hash and hot water for clean up. Ice chests or=20
                  Styrofoam=AE containers can be used for holding hot or =
cold=20
                  foods. Polyethylene liners help keep the containers =
sanitary,=20
                  attractive and easily cleaned. Food processors or =
grinders=20
                  greatly reduce the time required for preparing slaw, =
salads,=20
                  hash and other items requiring chopping or=20
                  cutting.<BR>Beverage service containers should be =
large enough=20
                  to hold the necessary amounts of hot and cold liquids, =
but=20
                  should also be easy to handle. Beverages available =
should=20
                  include water, milk and your main beverage. Allow =
extra of=20
                  each beverage for refills during the meal. Large, =
covered=20
                  coolers with spigots work best because there is room =
for ice=20
                  to be added to precook the liquids, reducing the =
amount of ice=20
                  needed in the cups. Hot coffee is best offered =
self-service=20
                  from the pot or an insulated thermos. Beverage =
concentrates=20
                  for tea, lemonade and juice work well for large =
groups, but=20
                  make sure a sanitary water supply is available.=20
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Food Safety</P>Plan for the =
proper=20
                  handling and preparation of all food items to prevent =
food=20
                  borne illness. Remember the following tips when =
handling food:=20

                  <UL>
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Keep perishable foods such =
as meat,=20
                    poultry and fresh vegetables refrigerated until they =
are=20
                    ready to be cooked or prepared.=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Thaw all frozen meats in the =

                    refrigerator, not at room temperature.=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Keep uncooked, cooked and =
prepared=20
                    foods covered as much of the time as possible. Use =
foil on=20
                    containers without lids.=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Always wash and sanitize the =
cutting=20
                    board, pans and lugs before reusing for cooked =
foods. Keep=20
                    all food containers and equipment clean.=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Provide hand-washing =
facilities at the=20
                    outdoor cooking sites and always keep your hands =
clean.=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Provide clean hot water for =
cleaning=20
                    during outdoor cooking. A fish fryer or gas stove =
can be=20
                    used to heat water for washing equipment and =
utensils.=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Don't handle food if you =
have cuts or=20
                    sores on your hands. Cover with a bandage and use =
gloves.=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Keep cold foods cold (below =
40 degrees=20
                    F) and hot foods hot (above 140 degrees F) when =
holding=20
                    before serving.=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Promptly chill leftovers to =
below 40=20
                    degrees F in shallow containers less than four =
inches deep=20
                    immediately after serving is complete.=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Protect the food by wearing =
clean=20
                    clothing and keeping your hair covered.=20
                    <LI class=3Dheadline_sm>Comply with local ordinances =

                    concerning food preparation. Restroom facilities are =

                    recommended for large groups, and may be rented. =
</LI></UL>
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Food Preparation - Cooking the=20
                  Meal</P>Timing is the key to food preparation. Prepare =
all the=20
                  food items so they are ready to serve hot or cold at =
the=20
                  appointed time. In order to achieve this, prepare and=20
                  refrigerate all items that can be assembled prior to =
cooking=20
                  the meat. Prepare salad or slaw ingredients =
separately, then=20
                  combine and mix with dressing just before serving. =
Hash or=20
                  Brunswick stew can be cooked the day before, placed in =
shallow=20
                  containers for rapid cooling, refrigerated and =
reheated just=20
                  before serving. Vegetables that require cooking, =
(baked=20
                  potatoes) can be held hot in thermal chests for up to =
two=20
                  hours before serving. Rice, grits, corn on the cob, =
hash,=20
                  green beans and baked beans can be cooked in fish =
fryers=20
                  on-site or prepared on a stove and held for up to one =
hour in=20
                  thermal chests before serving. Plan the menu to =
accommodate=20
                  the available facilities. Baked items should be baked =
ahead of=20
                  time or purchased from a bakery. Sheet cakes, =
brownies,=20
                  cupcakes and fruit pies work well. Avoid cream filled =
or=20
                  custard baked goods because they spoil easily.=20
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>The Fire</P>Barbecuing for =
large groups=20
                  is usually done over an open pit or in a large =
portable metal=20
                  grill, with a wood or charcoal fire. Burn wood down to =
form a=20
                  bed of hot coals, then place the coals in the pit for =
cooking.=20
                  Not all wood burns well, so select a good supply of =
dry=20
                  hardwood. Hickory, oak, pecan, maple and ash provide a =
good=20
                  bed of coals when burned, but soft-woods such as pine =
produce=20
                  a lot of smoke and off-flavors in the meat. Avoid damp =
or=20
                  green wood. Start the fire well ahead of the time you =
expect=20
                  to cook the meat so that a good bed of coals is =
available.=20
                  Keep a modest fire burning to ensure additional coals =
are=20
                  available as needed.<BR>Charcoal is the preferred heat =
source=20
                  and charcoal briquettes are the best form to use. =
Estimate the=20
                  amount of charcoal needed to cover the grill area or =
to form a=20
                  bank beside the carcass. Build a pyramid of briquettes =
and add=20
                  the lighter fluid. When the briquettes are covered =
with white=20
                  ash (about 20 minutes), the fire is ready for cooking. =
Do not=20
                  light all of the charcoal at once when cooking a large =
roast=20
                  or a whole pig, but supply the fire by adding =
briquettes to=20
                  the fire as needed. Use commercial lighter fluid or =
mineral=20
                  spirits to start a fire. Never use kerosene or other =
fuels=20
                  that are absorbed by the meat and produce off =
flavors.<BR>When=20
                  the charcoal is ready, spread it evenly around the =
grill. For=20
                  large pieces of meat or for whole carcasses, it is =
best to=20
                  place the fire parallel to the meat and not directly =
under it=20
                  to reduce flare fires. Always keep a spray bottle or =
water=20
                  source handy to extinguish any flare ups and prevent =
burning=20
                  the meat. Control the heat by removing or adding =
charcoal to=20
                  the fire. One firing of charcoal should be sufficient =
to cook=20
                  steaks or chops. A moderately hot fire is best for =
browning=20
                  ribs or cooking steaks or chops. By placing your hand =
over the=20
                  fire at the height of the meat, you can determine the =
amount=20
                  of heat: You can hold your hand over a hot fire to a =
count of=20
                  four or five and over a low fire to 10. A low fire =
provides=20
                  the heat necessary for barbecuing large cuts and whole =
pigs.=20
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Grill Cooking</P>Cooking =
steaks, chops or=20
                  other meat items on a grill requires some care. If the =
meat=20
                  sticks to the grill, grease the grill lightly. Clean =
off any=20
                  burned particles that can stick to the food. Turn the =
meat=20
                  only when necessary. Use tongs instead of a fork when =
turning=20
                  the meat. Do not salt until the meat is nearly done. =
To test=20
                  for doneness of steals, make a small split in the =
center with=20
                  a knife and check the center for color. Do not cook =
over too=20
                  high of a heat. Slow cooking results in a juicier =
product.=20
                  Wood chips can be added for a smoked flavor, but =
should be=20
                  soaked in water first to prevent flare-ups. Sawdust =
can also=20
                  be used if dampened before being sprinkled over the =
fire.=20
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Pit Roasting a Pig</P>A pig =
that is 75 to=20
                  200 pounds live weight can be cooked by either of two =
methods:=20
                  on a rotisserie over a low fire, or on screens or rods =
over a=20
                  low fire with hand turning of the pig.=20
                  <P>For cooking on a rotisserie, use a pit of adequate =
size=20
                  with windscreens to prevent the ashes from blowing and =
to=20
                  reduce heat loss. The drive motor should be large =
enough to=20
                  ensure proper turning. Do not split the pig into =
halves.=20
                  Secure the carcass to the drive rod with wire and =
skewers so=20
                  that it will not slip off during cooking. The carcass =
should=20
                  be 12 to 18 inches above the fire and should be basted =
with=20
                  liquid (see recipes, pages 8-10) to prevent the meat =
from=20
                  drying during cooking. A low to medium fire (coals =
only) is=20
                  recommended and cooking time will be six to eight =
hours for a=20
                  40-pound carcass, and 12 to 14 hours for a 120-pound =
carcass.=20
                  Use a meat thermo-meter to determine doneness (165 =
degrees F)=20
                  underneath the shoulder blade or in the center of the =
ham.=20
                  Remove the meat from the fire and add any other sauces =
or=20
                  seasonings after slicing.<BR>The second method is to =
cook the=20
                  pig over an open pit with the carcass on a wire screen =
or with=20
                  long rods run through the pig. This requires the pig =
to be=20
                  split in half down the backbone. The pit should be 16 =
inches=20
                  deep, and constructed of two layers of concrete block. =
The pit=20
                  should be 12 inches longer than the pig (about five =
feet). The=20
                  concrete block will support the screen handles or =
rods. An=20
                  area 40 inches wide is required to allow room on each =
side of=20
                  the pig. If screens are used, construct them from =
half-inch=20
                  pipe covered with a hardware screen of one-inch mesh. =
If rods=20
                  are used, two half-inch solid rods are run lengthwise =
down=20
                  each side with three 3/8-inch rods run across the pig =
through=20
                  the ham, middle and shoulder. The rods should be wired =
to the=20
                  larger rods to prevent the carcass from slipping and =
falling=20
                  off during cooking. Turn the carcass by placing one =
screen on=20
                  top of the other, grasping both handles and inverting =
quickly.=20
                  A minimum of three screens is required. Screens can =
also be=20
                  used for chicken halves. Start cooking the pig with =
the bone=20
                  side down. Cook in this position for at least 30 =
minutes, then=20
                  baste and turn. The carcass will require frequent =
basting and=20
                  turning to prevent drying and charring, and turning =
should be=20
                  done more frequently as the meat becomes done. Be sure =
to=20
                  check doneness with a meat thermometer (160 degrees F) =
in the=20
                  thickest portion.<BR>The meat should be carved while =
hot by=20
                  slicing across the muscle fibers. Thin cuts can be =
de-boned=20
                  and minced, and excess fat should be removed. Add =
barbecue=20
                  sauce and stir.<BR>A portable grill can be used for=20
                  barbecuing, with gas, mod or charcoal as the heat =
source.=20
                  Portable grills work well when cooking for groups of =
up to 150=20
                  people.</P>
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Roasting a Small Pig</P>A pig =
weighing 20=20
                  to 40 pounds live will yield about 25 percent of that =
weight=20
                  in edible meat. Have the pig dressed, but do not split =
the=20
                  breast bone. Remove the head, if desired, by cutting =
the neck=20
                  off smoothly. If the head is not removed, open the =
throat area=20
                  and remove the esophagus and windpipe at the base of =
the=20
                  tongue and wash the area thoroughly.<BR>Rub the body =
cavity=20
                  with salt and stuff loosely with oyster dressing just =
before=20
                  roasting. Use oyster dressing because meat from a =
small pig=20
                  has little flavor. Stuff the pig just before roasting, =
to=20
                  prevent food borne illness. Sew or skewer the opening =
shut.=20
                  Fold the legs tightly against the body and tie with =
wire.=20
                  Place the pig on a pit rod and secure firmly so that =
the=20
                  carcass will rotate. The pig should be approximately =
24 inches=20
                  above the pit with enough coals for a low-heat fire =
along each=20
                  side of the carcass. Baste the pig every 15 minutes =
with a=20
                  basting solution to prevent the skin from burning. =
Roast six=20
                  hours, reducing the heat slightly during the last =
hour, and=20
                  cook until the stuffing reaches an internal =
temperature of 165=20
                  degrees F. Remove the pig from the pit and remove the=20
                  stuffing. Place the pig on a large platter with the =
back up,=20
                  garnish, and cover until ready to serve.<BR>To carve, =
split=20
                  the skin down the middle of the back. Lay skin off to =
each=20
                  side. Scrape off subcutaneous fat, and then pick the =
meat off=20
                  the bones with a serving fork (the meat is too tender =
to=20
                  slice). Serve with cranberry sauce, candied sweet =
potatoes,=20
                  green beans, relishes, hot rolls and tossed or Waldorf =
salad.=20
                  For aesthetic value, roast the head separately, =
wrapped in=20
                  foil, and later reattached to the pig on the platter =
with=20
                  skewers and with an apple in its mouth.=20
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Pit Barbecuing =
Chicken</P>Broiler halves=20
                  or quarters are the best serving size for a meal. =
Start=20
                  cooking the chicken with the bone side down. Keep the =
fire low=20
                  so that the broiler will not scorch or dry out. Allow =
1-1/2 to=20
                  two hours for well-done barbecued chicken. The cooking =
time=20
                  will depend on the height of the pit (16 inches is=20
                  recommended), the size of the broiler, the heat of the =
fire=20
                  and the weather conditions. Mop or brush the chicken =
with=20
                  sauce each time the broiler is turned. When cooking =
for large=20
                  groups, the sauce can be sprayed on with a new, unused =
regular=20
                  garden sprayer. Turn the chicken with tongs every five =
to 10=20
                  minutes. For large groups, racks built of welded wire =
(like=20
                  those used for a pork side) turn easily by placing an =
empty=20
                  rack on top of the filled rack and turning. Check =
doneness by=20
                  twisting a thigh joint or a wing joint - when the bone =
twists=20
                  out easily and has no red appearance, the meat is =
done.=20
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Serving the Meal</P>When =
serving the=20
                  meal, personal appearance is important. Wear a clean =
hat and=20
                  apron, clean clothes and a smile. Wash your hands and =
wear=20
                  disposable plastic gloves.=20
                  <P>Use scoops, spoons and ladles to control the size =
of the=20
                  servings. Be sure portions are generous, but avoid =
waste.=20
                  Insulated containers, thermal chests and Styrofoam =
chests with=20
                  liners make excellent serving containers for hot and =
cold=20
                  foods. Rice, grits, hash and other hot foods can be =
served=20
                  from the cooking container if necessary. Keep all =
foods=20
                  covered before and after serving with plastic wrap, =
aluminum=20
                  foil or lids.<BR>The serving line layout should be =
designed so=20
                  all people are served in 30 to 45 minutes for large =
crowds=20
                  (250) and 15 to 20 minutes for small groups. The =
number of=20
                  serving lines required for prepared plates is one line =
for=20
                  each 100 people served for each 15 to 20 minutes of =
serving=20
                  time. Four lines serving the same items on all plates =
can=20
                  serve 600 people in 30 minutes, providing backup food =
items=20
                  for the lines are readily available and beverage =
service is=20
                  equally as fast. Plan and organize the serving line so =
that=20
                  each worker knows which item he is responsible for and =
where=20
                  and how to supply the item.</P>
                  <P class=3Dheadline_sm>Clean Up and Write Up</P>The =
best sign of=20
                  a successful event is when your excess barbecue is =
sold at a=20
                  profit to those attending. Always cover, seal and =
promptly=20
                  refrigerate all leftovers at 40 degrees F or below. =
Use=20
                  shallow pans or small containers (less than five =
pounds) to=20
                  ensure that the food chills rapidly. Dispose of any =
food=20
                  served family-style or self-service or that has been =
exposed=20
                  to anyone's hands.<BR>Provide trash containers for =
waste=20
                  removal. Clean up the cooking area and wet down ashes =
or other=20
                  waste wood before removing them. Wash all serving =
utensils,=20
                  pans, pots and cooking equipment in warm soapy water. =
Rinse in=20
                  hot water and sanitize with a bleach solution (1/2 =
ounce of=20
                  bleach in one gallon of water). Air dry to prevent the =
use of=20
                  a dirty towel or dirty hands, which can contaminate =
the clean=20
                  surfaces with bacteria. Remember, you are putting the =
public=20
                  at risk when these procedures are not followed. Write =
a=20
                  summary report detailing the number attending, the =
success or=20
                  failure of the event, food acceptance, the amount and =
type of=20
                  leftovers, shortages, costs and suggestions for future =
events.=20
                  Keep your worksheets for future reference.=20
                  <P>
