                    <TBODY>
                    <TR>
                      <TD colSpan=3D2>Table 1. Meat Selection Guide for =
Outdoor=20
                        Cooking</TD></TR>
                    <TR>
                      <TD vAlign=3Dtop>BEEF</TD>
                      <TD>Choose USDA Grade Prime, Choice or Select.=20
                        Recommended cuts are 1 to 1-1/2 inch thick steak =
from=20
                        the rib, loin or sirloin; or 8-10 pound roast =
from the=20
                        rib, round or rump. Cook to the desired degree =
of=20
                        doneness.</TD></TR>
                    <TR>
                      <TD vAlign=3Dtop>PORK</TD>
                      <TD>Whole hogs or pork sides should come from lean =
pigs=20
                        with less than 0.8 inch of backfat and good =
muscling.=20
                        Pork primals (hams, shoulders and butts) should =
be=20
                        well-trimmed. Avoid pale, soft and watery pork. =
Chops=20
                        and steaks are best cut thick (1 to 1-1/2 =
inches). Cured=20
                        pork is excellent char broiled.</TD></TR>
                    <TR>
                      <TD vAlign=3Dtop>LAMB</TD>
                      <TD>Choose well-trimmed cuts. All cuts are =
acceptable=20
                        because the animal is young. Chops should be =
thick.=20
                        Remove fat during preparation and cook over =
medium fire=20
                        to the desired doneness. Because lamb has a high =
melting=20
                        point for fat, serve hot.</TD></TR>
                    <TR>
                      <TD vAlign=3Dtop>GOAT</TD>
                      <TD>Select a kid or yearling that has been grain =
fed.=20
                        All cuts are acceptable. Bone and tie thin cuts =
to=20
                        prevent burning and drying out. Cook until well=20
                    done.</TD></TR>
                    <TR>
                      <TD vAlign=3Dtop>VENISON</TD>
                      <TD>Venison quality is determined by the care =
taken=20
                        during handling and processing of the carcass. =
Carefully=20
                        trim off an discoloration. Cuts can be rubbed =
with spice=20
                        and wrapped in foil to prevent excessive drying, =
because=20
                        venison has little or no fat cover. Basting =
during=20
                        cooking also prevents drying.</TD></TR>
                    <TR>
                      <TD vAlign=3Dtop>CHICKEN</TD>
                      <TD>Select fresh 2 to 2-1/2 pound broilers split =
into=20
                        halves or quarters. Remove fat pads and wash the =
meat in=20
                        cold water before cooking. Baste frequently =
during=20
                        cooking over a medium fire. Test doneness by =
twisting=20
                        the thigh or wing joint, which will separately =
easily=20
                        when done. Doneness can be checked in the thigh =
meat=20
                        with a thermometer. Cook to 180 degrees =
F.</TD></TR>
                    <TR>
                      <TD vAlign=3Dtop>SAUSAGE, FRESH</TD>
                      <TD>Franks, ring bologna, Polish kielbasa, smoked =
beef=20
                        or pork sausages and many other cooked sausages =
are=20
                        excellent for outdoor cooking, especially for =
small=20
                        groups or as an alternative meat item. Cook over =
a=20
                        medium fire to 145 degrees F. Use local sources =
for best=20
                        acceptance.</TD></TR>
                    <TR>
                      <TD vAlign=3Dtop>TURKEY</TD>
                      <TD>Select medium to small turkeys for barbecuing =
or=20
                        smoking. Be sure to use fresh, not frozen, =
birds. Remove=20
                        the neck and giblets and leave the body cavity =
open. Do=20
                        not stuff before cooking. Cook over a medium =
fire,=20
                        basting often to an internal temperature of 180 =
degrees=20
                        F at the inside of the thigh. Pre basted birds =
can be=20
                        used. Turkey breasts or other parts make an =
excellent=20
                        selection for barbecuing or grilling and should =
be=20
                        cooked to 180 degrees F for dark meat and 170 =
degrees F=20
                        for white meat.</TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE>
